        ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x+5)$ at the point where $x=2.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is given by ${f}^{\u2032}(2).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u,\\,u=3x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(2) & =3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3\\cdot 2+5) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}\u206111.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is $3\\operatorname{cos}\u206111.$"
      },
